  "query_preview": "..\nCurrently there is isolated to scattered showers across\nSoutheastern Arizona, with the most activity in areas east of \nTucson in Cochise, Graham, Santa Cruz and SE Pinal counties. \nRainfall accumulations have been light with less than 0.10\ninches. The latest CAMS are struggling to get a handle on the \ntiming and placement of this activity, however this activity\nshould move north/northeastward out of the area by mid morning. \nSince the CAMs are struggling with this activity, it still has \nshowers/thunderstorms today beginning in the mid afternoon, \nhowever depending on how much cloud cover can exit the region \nthe start for any activity can be delayed. Best chances for \nactivity is where the best instability is areas south and east \nof Tucson. Locally heavy rainfall with isolated flash flooding \nremains to be the primary impact with stronger storms.\n\nStorm activity and coverage will slowly increase this week, with\nthe more active days looks to be Wednesday and Thursday as the\narea of high pressure moves eastward overhead, introducing more\ndaytime heating and instability, as well as easterly to\nnortheasterly flow aloft. The available moisture continues to\nhave flash flooding as the primary threat, however stronger\nstorms during this time can be capable of strong outflow winds\nas well.\n\nTemperatures will be within a couple of degrees of normal through \nThursday, then dropping to below normal levels through the\nweekend into early next week.",
